# Rank 1514 / CVE-2026-7551: OpenHarness registered its bridge command as
# remotely invocable by default, so a gateway caller could spawn bridge
# sessions on the victim's machine (missing function-level access control,
# CWE-306). The SlashCommand dataclass in
# src/openharness/commands/registry.py declares `remote_invocable: bool =
# True`, so every command create_default_command_registry registers is
# callable through the remote gateway unless the registration opts out, and
# the "bridge" registration passed no exposure flags while its handler
# inspects and spawns bridge sessions (the gateway test shipped with the fix
# asserts a remote "/bridge spawn" message never reaches the handler). The
# fixing commit 438e37309 ("fix: keep bridge command local-only by default")
# adds `remote_invocable=False, remote_admin_opt_in=True` to that one
# registration, aligning it with the four commands (plugin, reload-plugins,
# permissions, plan) that already opted out at the parent.
#
# ## What fires, both revisions
#
# Three --cache off runs per revision over src/ (222 analysed files) report
# **477 findings each, deterministically, identically on the vulnerable
# parent 380bab4 and the fixed 438e373** -- 21 rule IDs, byte-identical rule
# histograms, zero findings on the command table (registry.py:1840-1879) in
# any run, and the string SlashCommand appears in no finding at either
# revision. The full findings view was read, not the `-flags` only view. The
# only auth row the commands module produces is VYQL-AUTH-105 on
# code.RateLimitRequiredOp at registry.py:120 (CommandRegistry.lookup) and
# :768 (_login_handler), an authentication-throttle heuristic far from the
# registration; the file's other rows are InsecurePermission mkdirs, UrlFetch
# and FilePathAccess pairs, likewise unrelated. The 1st pass's zero was real.
#
# ## Why nothing ships
#
# The weakness is a registration default, not a flow: no tainted value
# reaches a sink, the handler never runs unless a remote peer asks, and the
# whole fix is two keyword arguments on a constructor call.
#
# * The only shipped remote-exposure vocabulary is
#   RemoteMethodCodeExecutionMissingAuthorization, anchored on Meteor's
#   documented `Meteor.methods` contract ("invoked over the network by
#   clients", javascript). OpenHarness' registry is application-internal
#   wiring: grep of the project's docs, README and CHANGELOG finds no
#   statement of the remote_invocable default at all, so there is no
#   documentation for a binding to answer to.
# * The powerful operation the exposure reaches -- `get_bridge_manager()
#   .spawn(...)` inside the bridge handler -- is already labelled
#   code.ResourceReview by auditreview.marks, but no rule in packs/ consumes
#   that concept, so it produces no finding; the missing half is the
#   registration-side exposure fact, and writing it means naming this
#   application's own SlashCommand constructor and remote_invocable flag.
#   That detects this application rather than the weakness, and the flag
#   spelling is the fixing commit's own vocabulary.
# * The generic alternative -- a registration-shaped call that omits a
#   security flag -- fires on every register/add call in every Python tree;
#   the no-signal-in-the-shape family was measured unshippable at 1331 and
#   1417, and the credential-versus-decision siblings 1501, 1505, 1508, 1512
#   and 1513 are held on the same ground. The one sibling with a documented
#   convention to anchor on (1504, the `dangerously*` schema-override
#   spellings over a library schema-builder API) converted; this registry has
#   no convention and no library API.
#
# Recorded as a vocabulary gap of the exposure-defaults family; no
# ENGINE-GAPS.md entry -- every construct in the file parses, resolves and
# labels normally, so nothing engine-side blocks a future binding. No
# definition change shipped.
#
# ## Recheck measurements (knowledge base tip f065d428d)
#
# The A/B above is at the span tip including the tool-schema-override rule;
# an earlier 3+3 at tip 8ff93bbea reported the same 477/477 split, so the
# later-shipped vocabulary changed nothing on this tree. Narrow spec 5/5
# passing with -v. Rank stays ATTENTION.

test "rank1514: the bridge command is registered with the table's default exposure"
  lang python
  file src/openharness/commands/registry.py
  reject VYQL-AUTH-105
  code
  ```
  from dataclasses import dataclass

  @dataclass
  class SlashCommand:
      name: str
      description: str
      handler: CommandHandler
      remote_invocable: bool = True
      remote_admin_opt_in: bool = False
      aliases: tuple[str, ...] = ()

  def create_default_command_registry(plugin_commands=None):
      registry = CommandRegistry()
      registry.register(SlashCommand("rewind", "Remove the latest conversation turn(s)", _rewind_handler))
      registry.register(SlashCommand("files", "List files in the current workspace", _files_handler))
      registry.register(SlashCommand("init", "Initialize project OpenHarness files", _init_handler))
      registry.register(SlashCommand("bridge", "Inspect bridge helpers and spawn bridge sessions", _bridge_handler))
      registry.register(SlashCommand("login", "Show auth status or store an API key", _login_handler))
      registry.register(
          SlashCommand(
              "plugin",
              "Manage plugins",
              _plugin_handler,
              remote_invocable=False,
              remote_admin_opt_in=True,
          )
      )
      return registry
  ```

